1
00:00:02,281 --> 00:00:04,980
So now that we know how to name variables,

2
00:00:05,076 --> 00:00:07,614
constants, and properties, let's see more

3
00:00:07,679 --> 00:00:10,008
examples and let's see different variations

4
00:00:10,008 --> 00:00:10,442
of names.

5
00:00:11,277 --> 00:00:13,141
For that, we will consider what's being

6
00:00:13,141 --> 00:00:15,904
stored. We'll then have a look at bad names,

7
00:00:16,482 --> 00:00:19,278
at okayish names, and at good names.

8
00:00:20,354 --> 00:00:22,185
And let's start with the good old user

9
00:00:22,346 --> 00:00:25,061
object again. Let's say we have a variable

10
00:00:25,093 --> 00:00:27,583
in which we store a user object. And the

11
00:00:27,583 --> 00:00:30,084
same would be true if it's a constant or if

12
00:00:30,084 --> 00:00:30,854
it's a property.

13
00:00:31,432 --> 00:00:33,436
And let's say that user object is simply the

14
00:00:33,436 --> 00:00:35,505
combination of the name of the user, the

15
00:00:35,586 --> 00:00:36,933
email address, and the age.

16
00:00:37,687 --> 00:00:39,162
Now, what could be bad names here?

17
00:00:40,349 --> 00:00:42,642
Now, bad names for this kind of variable

18
00:00:42,899 --> 00:00:45,064
would be something like u or data.

19
00:00:45,930 --> 00:00:48,737
u is simply very short and unspecific, and

20
00:00:48,913 --> 00:00:51,608
data is also not very specific. These

21
00:00:51,640 --> 00:00:53,885
variables could contain anything. It's not

22
00:00:53,885 --> 00:00:56,451
clear that they hold some user object.

23
00:00:57,398 --> 00:01:00,623
Names which are okay could be userData

24
00:01:00,703 --> 00:01:03,286
instead of just data or person.

25
00:01:04,217 --> 00:01:07,121
The problem with userData is that it might

26
00:01:07,121 --> 00:01:09,592
have some redundant information attached to

27
00:01:09,689 --> 00:01:12,577
it. I mean, if we have a user object, it's

28
00:01:12,577 --> 00:01:15,385
clear that it contains data about that user.

29
00:01:15,706 --> 00:01:17,984
So we probably don't wanna name the variable

30
00:01:18,177 --> 00:01:18,834
userData.

31
00:01:19,861 --> 00:01:22,332
And for person, that might be too

32
00:01:22,348 --> 00:01:25,510
unspecific. It can make sense. I will not

33
00:01:25,510 --> 00:01:26,666
say that it's always wrong,

34
00:01:27,244 --> 00:01:30,037
but often you might be working on a customer

35
00:01:30,374 --> 00:01:33,730
or on an author or an instructor or an

36
00:01:33,826 --> 00:01:36,299
administrator. So maybe you wanna use these

37
00:01:36,411 --> 00:01:37,583
specific versions then.

38
00:01:38,241 --> 00:01:41,147
If we would not be storing a user object

39
00:01:41,147 --> 00:01:43,828
here, but let's say we would just be

40
00:01:44,262 --> 00:01:47,216
collecting data a user entered in some form

41
00:01:47,328 --> 00:01:49,865
and we have a mixture of the entered values

42
00:01:50,363 --> 00:01:53,796
and of validation results of us validating

43
00:01:53,828 --> 00:01:54,470
these inputs,

44
00:01:55,015 --> 00:01:57,726
then we could have a userData variable which

45
00:01:57,838 --> 00:02:00,966
holds this bag of entered values and

46
00:02:00,966 --> 00:02:03,532
validation results. There, something like

47
00:02:03,532 --> 00:02:06,066
that could make more sense because it would

48
00:02:06,082 --> 00:02:08,969
not be the finished user object then, but

49
00:02:08,969 --> 00:02:12,033
some intermediate object between collecting

50
00:02:12,097 --> 00:02:14,070
the values and creating the user.

51
00:02:14,744 --> 00:02:16,893
So that's something to consider. And I'm

52
00:02:16,925 --> 00:02:19,331
bringing this up here just to make it clear

53
00:02:19,716 --> 00:02:22,009
that a name which might be okay in one

54
00:02:22,073 --> 00:02:24,478
scenario might be a good name in another

55
00:02:24,543 --> 00:02:26,579
scenario and vice versa.

56
00:02:27,253 --> 00:02:30,364
Just the bad names are really often very

57
00:02:30,460 --> 00:02:30,684
bad.

58
00:02:32,015 --> 00:02:34,613
So therefore good names could be user or as

59
00:02:34,645 --> 00:02:36,970
I just said something more specific like

60
00:02:37,051 --> 00:02:37,548
customer.

61
00:02:38,157 --> 00:02:40,402
User is pretty descriptive and clear and

62
00:02:40,418 --> 00:02:43,385
customer is even more specific which often

63
00:02:43,385 --> 00:02:45,630
might be the better option to make it really

64
00:02:45,694 --> 00:02:47,843
clear which kind of user that is.

65
00:02:48,436 --> 00:02:50,296
Of course, if you have an application where

66
00:02:50,505 --> 00:02:53,071
a user is really just a user and not

67
00:02:53,071 --> 00:02:55,460
necessarily a customer yet, then there is

68
00:02:55,572 --> 00:02:57,737
nothing wrong with going with just user,

69
00:02:58,042 --> 00:02:59,789
which is why it's listed under good names

70
00:02:59,806 --> 00:02:59,934
here.

71
00:03:01,185 --> 00:03:03,478
And with that, I got a first little exercise

72
00:03:03,574 --> 00:03:03,975
for you.

73
00:03:04,632 --> 00:03:06,941
Let's say we're now validating some user

74
00:03:06,957 --> 00:03:09,812
input and it's the result of this validation

75
00:03:09,908 --> 00:03:12,554
which is being stored in a variable or in a

76
00:03:12,554 --> 00:03:14,670
property. How would you name that?

77
00:03:15,680 --> 00:03:18,020
Pause the video for a second and think about

78
00:03:18,020 --> 00:03:20,730
bad, okay, and good names, and then we'll

79
00:03:20,730 --> 00:03:22,669
have a look at the examples I came up with.

80
00:03:23,647 --> 00:03:25,603
So what could be some bad names for this?

81
00:03:26,340 --> 00:03:28,648
Well, again, just using a single character

82
00:03:28,777 --> 00:03:31,277
is pretty much always bad with some rare

83
00:03:31,390 --> 00:03:34,612
exceptions. So we could use v for the

84
00:03:34,612 --> 00:03:35,606
validation result

85
00:03:36,199 --> 00:03:37,321
or something like val.

86
00:03:38,122 --> 00:03:39,886
The problem with v is that it could be

87
00:03:39,966 --> 00:03:42,466
anything. The problem with val is that we

88
00:03:42,547 --> 00:03:44,759
could also think that it stands for value

89
00:03:45,160 --> 00:03:46,892
instead of validationResult.

90
00:03:47,598 --> 00:03:50,181
So these are too unspecific and don't tell

91
00:03:50,181 --> 00:03:50,838
us anything.

92
00:03:51,913 --> 00:03:54,127
When it comes to okay names, we could have

93
00:03:54,159 --> 00:03:56,806
something like correct or validatedInput.

94
00:03:57,704 --> 00:04:00,271
The problem with these names is that they

95
00:04:00,271 --> 00:04:03,191
don't imply that they hold true or false

96
00:04:03,239 --> 00:04:03,688
values.

97
00:04:04,346 --> 00:04:06,944
Correct could be holding any kind of value.

98
00:04:07,169 --> 00:04:09,752
It could be true or false, but it could also

99
00:04:09,816 --> 00:04:12,399
be some text or anything else. And

100
00:04:12,543 --> 00:04:15,159
validatedInput could hold the value which

101
00:04:15,272 --> 00:04:17,599
was validated instead of the validation

102
00:04:17,599 --> 00:04:18,016
result.

103
00:04:18,979 --> 00:04:22,061
So better options could be isCorrect or

104
00:04:22,318 --> 00:04:24,003
isValid or something like

105
00:04:24,179 --> 00:04:26,811
didValidateSuccessfully. These would be

106
00:04:26,811 --> 00:04:29,315
names which are descriptive and where it's

107
00:04:29,315 --> 00:04:31,642
clear that we have a true or false value

108
00:04:31,642 --> 00:04:32,509
stored in there.

109
00:04:33,231 --> 00:04:34,933
Now, there will be more examples throughout

110
00:04:34,933 --> 00:04:36,939
this course, but this hopefully gives you a

111
00:04:36,939 --> 00:04:40,293
first idea on how variables, properties, and

112
00:04:40,309 --> 00:04:42,019
constants may be named.
